Docker — Introduction
Docker is an open-source platform that packages your application with all its dependencies (libraries, runtime, config) into a single portable unit called a container — so it runs identically on your laptop, your test server, and production. 

It directly solves the problem you saw in environment management: "works on my machine." 

Core Concepts
Term	What it is	Analogy
Dockerfile	A text file with instructions to build an image	Like a Git commit message + recipe
Image	A read-only blueprint (code + deps + config)	Like a Git repo
Container	A running instance of an image	Like a Git checkout
Docker Hub	A registry to store/share images	Like GitHub for images
Docker Engine	The daemon that builds and runs containers	The runtime

Container vs Virtual Machine
Docker Container	Virtual Machine
Virtualizes	OS-level (shares host kernel)	Full hardware (each has its own OS)
Size	~100 MBs	~GBs
Boot time	Milliseconds	Minutes
Isolation	Process-level (lighter)	Full OS-level (stronger)
Use case	Microservices, CI/CD, cloud-native	Legacy apps, multi-tenant, compliance

How It Connects to What You Already Know
DevOps concept	Docker's role
Environment parity	Same image in dev → staging → prod. No rebuild.
CI/CD pipeline	Pipeline builds the image → pushes to registry → deploys the same artifact to each env
IaC	Dockerfile is your "infrastructure as code" for the app layer
Git	Dockerfile is version-controlled in Git; image tags mirror commit hashes

Minimal Example
# Dockerfile
FROM node:18
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm install
CMD ["node", "server.js"]

docker build -t myapp .        # build the image
docker run -p 3000:3000 myapp  # run the container

In One Sentence
Docker lets you build once, run anywhere — the same container image that works on your laptop is the exact same one running in production, eliminating environment mismatch entirely.

docker command 
Command                                	                                                    What it does
docker ps	                                                                     Lists currently running containers (ID, image, name, status, ports).
docker images	                                                                 Lists all images stored locally on your machine (name, tag, size, creation date).
docker ps -a	                                                                 Lists all containers — running and stopped. The -a flag means "all".
docker stop <container id>	                                                   Stops a running container (sends a graceful shutdown signal).
docker start <container id>                                                    Starts a previously stopped container (without recreating it).
docker rm <container id>	                                                     Removes a stopped container from your system (frees the name/ID).
docker rmi <image name>	                                                       Removes an image from your local machine.
docker build -t mern-backend .                                                 Builds an image from the Dockerfile in the current directory (.) and tags it mern-backend.
docker build -t front-backend .	                                               Same — builds and tags another image (likely your frontend).
docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name=backend mern-backend	                       Runs the mern-backend image as a container in detached mode (-d), maps port 5000 on your host to port 5000 in the container, and names it backend.
docker run -d -p 5173:5173 --name=frontend mern-frontend	                     Same for the frontend — maps host port 5173 to container port 5173, names it frontend.
docker compose up -d	                                                         Starts all services defined in your docker-compose.yml file in detached mode. This is the modern way to run a multi-container app (e.g., frontend + backend + database) in one command. 

Quick Reference: Container Lifecycle
build → run → ps (running) → stop → ps -a (stopped) → start (again) → rm (delete)   

  docker run flags cheat sheet
Flag	      Meaning
-d        	Run in detached mode (background)
-p          host:container	Port mapping — expose a container port to your host
--name	    Give the container a readable name instead of a random ID

docker compose vs individual docker run
Approach	When to use
docker run (one at a time)	Single container, quick testing
docker compose up -d	Multi-service apps (frontend + backend + DB + cache) — defined in one docker-compose.yml

docker compose is the preferred approach for real projects because it handles networking, shared volumes, and service dependencies automatically.
  
